  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Zenodo software release has a small but clear evidence trail: it is openly available, linked to a GitHub source release, and already shows one documented downstream reuse in a biomedical article. The citation record is still at zero, so the impact story is early and grounded more in reuse and stewardship than in scholarly citation uptake.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">wsgriggs2/rt_fUS_BMI v0.1.0 is the first stable release of real-time fUS-BMI code, archived on Zenodo in 2023 by Whitney Griggs as an open software record. The metadata ties the release to a corresponding GitHub version and positions it as companion code for work on decoding motor plans using a closed-loop ultrasonic brain-machine interface. That combination of archival DOI, version linkage, and open access makes the software citable and reproducible in a way that is appropriate for a research codebase.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest downstream signal is reuse rather than citation. The record shows one documented reuse, appearing as a biomedical article mention in Europe PMC, which indicates that the software has already been taken up in the literature as part of related scientific work. At the same time, OpenAlex currently shows zero citations, and scite reports no citing or contrasting papers, so the broader scholarly citation footprint has not yet accumulated in the indexed sources available here.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Stewardship signals are solid. Zenodo provides persistent access and long-term archiving, and the release is openly accessible through OpenAIRE-matched instances. The software\u2019s concept profile also aligns with its technical role, emphasizing code, computer science, decoding methods, algorithms, and interface-related themes rather than a broader topical spread.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">What this record supports, then, is an early-stage impact narrative centered on preservation, openness, and initial research reuse. The evidence does not yet show citation momentum, but it does show that the code has been formally released, archived, and at least once incorporated into subsequent biomedical discussion.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Because event data are unavailable and no social or community discourse is recorded in the provided sources, there is no basis here for claims about public attention beyond the documented reuse and repository presence. The best-supported conclusion is that this is a reproducible software contribution with a modest but concrete footprint in the scholarly record.</p>",
